    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Lumbar disc herniation with left L5 radiculopathy (M51.16)</content>. Radiculopathy resolved: no leg pain and a normal neurological examination. No imaging or surgical opinion is needed. [S]<list><item>Home exercise program every morning; a graded return to jogging.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic low back pain (M54.59)</content>. Pain 6 to 3 out of 10 and Oswestry 36% to 18% with exercise therapy and without pain tablets since the sick visit. [S]<list><item>Continue the home program; heat after long drives.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Dyspepsia from naproxen (R10.13)</content>. Settling off naproxen and on omeprazole; Helicobacter pylori negative; no alarm features. [S]<list><item>Finish four weeks of omeprazole, then stop.</item><item>No oral anti-inflammatories; return if symptoms come back off omeprazole.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Overweight (E66.3)</content>. Down 2.6 kg (2.8%) in eight weeks with smaller portions and daily exercise. [S]<list><item>Continue; aim for 5% to 7% loss over six months.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Back pain intensity (0 to 10)</td><td>Every visit while treatment is adjusted</td><td>Completed today</td><td>3 out of 10, from 6 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Back function (Oswestry Disability Index)</td><td>At intake and every four to six weeks during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>18%, from 36% at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Leg neurological examination and red flags (weakness, saddle numbness, bladder or bowel change, fever, weight loss)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Normal; no red flags</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Lumbar imaging</td><td>Only for red flags, a progressive deficit, or when surgery is considered</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Radiculopathy resolved</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Exercise therapy (physical therapy, then a home program)</td><td>From diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Six weeks of physical therapy finished; home program every morning</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Oral NSAID safety: dose and frequency actually taken, stomach symptoms, blood pressure, kidney function</td><td>Every visit while an oral NSAID is used</td><td>Deferred</td><td>At the preventive visit three weeks ago he said he was taking naproxen most days, beyond the as-needed plan, and this was not acted on: no review of the dose, the need for it, or stomach protection. He developed dyspepsia about two weeks before this visit, without bleeding (hemoglobin 15.0 g/dL). Corrective action: naproxen stopped, omeprazole for four weeks, and the dose actually taken is now checked at every visit for any oral NSAID. No oral NSAID is now in use</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>No opioids or muscle relaxants</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Thumb base pain (0 to 10)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>3 out of 10, from 5</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Hand function (FIHOA, recorded in the note) and grip strength</td><td>At diagnosis and with each treatment trial</td><td>Completed today</td><td>FIHOA 6 out of 30, from 9; grip not repeated until the twelve-week visit</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Thumb joint examination (tenderness, grind, range, pinch)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Mild tenderness and grind</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Thumb orthosis and hand exercise program</td><td>From diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Splint at night and for gripping work; hand exercises daily</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Topical diclofenac: use and skin</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Twice a day; no skin irritation</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Clinic blood pressure, average of two seated readings</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>124/78 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Home blood pressure monitoring (validated upper-arm cuff)</td><td>To confirm an elevated reading; then every three to six months while elevated</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Next week of home readings before the twelve-week visit</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and BMI</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>89.4 kg, BMI 27.9</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Diet and physical activity (150 to 300 minutes a week)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Walking daily, swimming three times a week and slow jogging; about 240 minutes a week</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Brain health symptom inventory (headache, memory, attention, mood, behavior, sleep, balance) and neurological examination</td><td>Baseline, then yearly after a career with repetitive head impacts</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No new symptoms</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Leg pain gone and back pain at 3 or less out of 10, with an Oswestry index under 20%. Outcome by 1956-09-29: Back pain 6 to 3 out of 10 and Oswestry 36% to 18% at eight weeks; no leg pain [S]</item><item>Grip and pinch without thumb pain at 3 or less out of 10. In progress; target 1956-10-27 [S]</item><item>Lose 5% to 7% of body weight over six months with regular exercise. In progress; target 1957-02-04 [S]</item><item>Blood pressure under 120/80 mmHg at home without medicine. In progress; target 1956-10-27 [S]</item><item>Keep a documented brain health baseline and repeat it yearly. In progress; target 1957-08-04 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Noninvasive Treatments for Acute, Subacute, and Chronic Low Back Pain: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2017); 2019 American College of Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Osteoarthritis of the Hand, Hip, and Knee (2019); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, rheumatoid arthritis/osteoarthritis, hypertension and hyperlipidemia monographs (2026); National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke Consensus Diagnostic Criteria for Traumatic Encephalopathy Syndrome (2021); 2025 AHA/ACC/AANP/AAPA/ABC/ACCP/ACPM/AGS/AMA/ASPC/NMA/PCNA/SGIM Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults (2025); Physical Activity Guidelines for Americans, 2nd edition (2018); ACG and CAG Clinical Guideline: Management of Dyspepsia (2017)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Twelve-week review at the end of October, with a week of home blood pressure readings before it. Second hepatitis B dose and influenza vaccine then. Next scheduled visit 1956-10-27: twelve-week review: back and thumb on home programs, weight, blood pressure, mood, anxiety and sleep, and the plan ahead. [S]</paragraph></text>

